"""An app-started persona session that outlives the request that started it.

A chat turn (`agent_common.cli.ask`) is one HTTP request, one subprocess, one
stream: the request handler owns the whole thing, and the process dies when the handler returns
or the client disconnects. That is wrong for a session the APP starts — a
document arrives and a persona starts reading it — because nothing asked a
question and nobody is necessarily watching (`docs/design/agent-jobs.md`).
This module is that other shape: an `asyncio.Task`, independent of any
request, whose every event is persisted as it lands (`jobs_models.py`), so a
caller can open the job at any time and read it from the start, then follow
it until its run settles.

The job row is the bus (`docs/design/agent-jobs.md` §Any worker). A
deployment runs several worker processes and a job's CLI is a child of
exactly one of them, so nothing process-local can answer a request that
lands on another: `watch()` reads the event log forward from the database,
`send_message()` queues onto the row, `stop()` flags the row, and the
running process's own control loop (`_control`) acts on both. That process
keeps a heartbeat on the row; a `running` job whose heartbeat has gone stale
has no process behind it, which is how every worker tells a dead run from a
live one held by a sibling.

The CLI is driven the way it measures (2.1.283): with `--input-format
stream-json` it ignores a prompt on the command line and reads each turn as
a `user` frame on stdin, stays alive after a turn's `result` for as long as
stdin is open, and exits once stdin closes and every frame already read has
been answered. So the prompt and every later message are frames on stdin,
`--replay-user-messages` has the CLI echo each one back as the turn that
consumes it starts — which is also how a watcher sees both sides of the
conversation — and `_control` closes stdin once a `result` has arrived and
every frame written has been echoed.

A job belongs to a workspace (`AgentJob`/`AgentJobEvent` are
`WorkspaceScoped`, `docs/design/agent-jobs.md` §Tenancy). Every session this
module opens is created OUTSIDE a request — `db_session_factory()` is the
app's plain, engine-bound sessionmaker, never `SessionDep`'s request-scoped
one, because a job's writes must outlive the one request that triggered them
— so every function below that touches the database calls
`db.scoping.bind_session()` on its own freshly-opened session BEFORE it
touches either model. `authorize()` and the orphan sweep are the exceptions,
reading with `unscoped()` because the workspace is exactly what the first is
deciding whether to trust and the second spans every workspace.
"""

